Overview
--------

This tree contains unit tests.

To compile and run the tests, do 'make check' (either in the top folder,
or just in the folder containing the tests you're interested in).

IMPORTANT: To compile the tests, you need to have the "Check" unit
testing framework[1] installed.[2] If you have it installed, you will see
"Unit tests: yes" in configure's summary message; if you don't see this
message, you won't be able to compile the tests.[3]

Tips and tricks
---------------

* To be able to step with the debugger into test code, see [4]. E.g., do:

    $ export CK_FORK=no

[1]: https://libcheck.github.io/check/
[2]: Your package manager likely has it.
[3]: Actually, some tests (like src/vfs/extfs/helpers-list) don't use
     this framework and will compile just fine. But that's the exception.
[4]: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1649814/debugging-unit-test-in-c-using-check

Terminal tools integration checks
---------------------------------

After building and installing MC, run:

    MC_TEST_BINARY=/absolute/path/to/installed/mc \
        python3 tests/integration/test_terminal_tools.py -v
    python3 tests/integration/test_tool_lifecycle.py -v
    python3 tests/integration/test_session_names.py -v

These PTY checks use temporary configuration and fake tool executables. They
exercise background discovery, conditional actions, session selection, new
sessions, selected directories, shell quoting, nested tmux, the detach status
hint, and the nano default without accessing real sessions or assistant services.

In MC, the Tools menu separates daily use from maintenance:
- Sessions: list, attach, create, or manage running tmux sessions.
- Assistants: launch installed assistants in a new tmux session.
- Install / update / purge: select any tool, then its lifecycle action or details.

Manage running sessions offers Attach, Rename, Detach all clients (programs keep
running), and Stop session (ends running programs). Stop and Detach require a
confirmation; Cancel is the initial selection. Session IDs, not interpolated
names, identify the selected session.

Purge means program AND settings, credentials, and history. A preview lists the
paths before confirmation. Claude Code and Codex log out before uninstalling to
clear OS credential-store entries; a failed logout stops the purge. tmux cannot
be purged while its server has running sessions. Unknown installation methods,
symlinked data paths, and data paths outside HOME fail explicitly rather than
claiming a full purge. Shared package caches and unrelated tools are preserved.
The operation runs with terminal progress/error output and waits for Enter
before returning to MC. Tool discovery restarts afterwards.

Fresh Pi, OpenCode, and Codex installs use their official npm packages in private
prefixes under ~/.local/share/mymc/tools/<tool>. npm/Node.js are prerequisites
(Pi needs Node.js 22.19+). Hermes and Claude Code use their official HTTPS
installers. curl and bash are prerequisites for those installers. tmux uses the
system package manager (apt, dnf, pacman, zypper, or Homebrew) and requests admin
rights through sudo where needed. Existing recognized npm, native, Homebrew,
and Pi Nix installations update/uninstall through their original method.
Native Pi purge invokes its official installer: choose Uninstall Pi there; MC
checks that the program was removed before deleting user data.

The tests use fake package managers and HTTPS downloads: they do not download
or install vendor tools, modify a real package database, or delete real user
credentials. They also verify cancellation, failed installs/logouts, exact
session selection, and purge path boundaries.
New assistant sessions are named `pi-00`, `claude-code-00`, `hermes-00`,
`opencode-00`, or `codex-cli-00`, using the lowest available number for that
assistant. Both Codex executable names share the `codex-cli` sequence. Numbers
expand beyond 99; up to 9999 is supported. Existing sessions and titles are not
changed, and ordinary shell sessions keep tmux's default naming.

New sessions use a selected local directory, otherwise the active local panel
directory, otherwise home. Claude Code is detected as `claude`; Codex CLI is
`codex` (or `codex-cli` when `codex` is unavailable). tmux is required for launch
actions. TerminalTools, TerminalSessions, TerminalAssistants, and TerminalManage
can also be assigned in a custom keymap.

Sessions opened through MC show `Detach: Ctrl+b, then d (session keeps running)`
in the tmux status line, using the configured prefix if it differs. Hold Ctrl
and press b, release both keys, then press d to return to MC. Existing status
text is retained after the hint. Existing explicit editor preferences and EDITOR
still take precedence over the new nano default.
